# When treatment arrives at different times: staggered DiD in trouble
subtitle: "Why two-way fixed effects can report harm when every state gained, and how clean comparisons, and one careful Medicaid study, repair it."
tags: ["difference-in-differences","staggered adoption","two-way fixed effects","medicaid expansion","event study"]
teaches: ["methods/difference-in-differences","methods/event-study","concepts/negative-weights"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow"}
next: {"title":"Regression discontinuity: what happens at your 65th birthday","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/regression-discontinuity-what-happens-at-your-65th-birthday.cast"}

## Two states, one regression
voice: male
level: advanced
text: {"math_hand":false}

