    card title "Two states, one regression"

Suppose every state that adopts a policy gains from it, and the regression we all learned reports that the policy did harm.
    draw h13 h14 h15 rule_h

That can happen, and it happens for one reason: the states adopted in different years.
    draw time_arr

Medicaid grew that way under the Affordable Care Act: after a Supreme Court  (@highlight h14 effect underline color #e3a21a@)ruling in 2012, each state chose whether, (@highlight h15 effect underline color #e3a21a@) and when, to expand.

So take two made-up states. Early expands in 2 (@draw l_name l_sub@)014, and Late waits until 2015.
    draw e_name e_sub

The outcome is the share of low-income adults with insu (@draw e13@)rance. Early goes from sixty, t (@draw e14@)o sixty-four, to seven (@draw e15@)ty-two percent.

Late goes from fi (@draw l13 l14@)fty to fifty-two, and then to fifty-six in its fir (@draw l15@)st expanded year.

Red mar (@highlight e13 l13 l14@)ks a year before a state expands, (@highlight e14 e15 l15@) blue a year after, and the blue cells f (@draw stair@)orm a staircase. That staircase is the whole problem.

Because these states are made up, we know what real data never shows: without expansion, each wo (@draw trend@)uld have gained two points a year anyway.
    erase stair

So expansion added two points in (@draw t_e14@) Early's first year, eight in it (@draw t_e15@)s second as people kept enrolling, and two in (@draw t_l15@) Late's first year.

Every effect is positive, and across the three expanded cells they average four percentage points.
    draw rule_a avg_lab avg_val

    erase trend

The standard tool is two-way fixed effects: a dummy for each state, a dummy for each year, and one coefficient, beta, on being expanded.
    draw eq

The sta (@highlight eq part "{\\alpha_g}" effect underline color #e3a21a@)te dummies soak up that Early simply has more coverage; the year dummie (@highlight eq part "{\\lambda_t}" effect underline color #e3a21a@)s soak up the two points a year that everyone gains.

    quiz "Every effect in the table is positive. What will two-way fixed effects report for $\\hat\\beta$?" intro "First, a guess."
        right: Below zero: it comes out at minus one, though no state lost anything.
        wrong: Ask what the regression can use as a control in 2015, when both states have expanded.
        * About 4, the average
        * Somewhere between 2 and 8
        + Below zero

Run it on these six numbers and beta-hat is minus one:  (@draw bhat ends@)expansion, says the regression, cut coverage by a percentage point.

Yet no state lost a single point. So th (@highlight t_e14 t_e15 t_l15@)e regression is comparing something other than what we meant, and the way to find out what is to take beta-hat apart.
    draw bmark

## Taking the estimate apart
voice: male
level: advanced
vars:
    b:
      value: 8
      min: 0
      max: 10
      step: 1
      label: Early's 2015 effect
    yE:
      expr: 64 + b
    c2:
      expr: 4 - b
    tw:
      expr: 3 - b/2
text: {"math_hand":false}
sources:
    - id: gb2021
      title: Difference-in-differences with variation in treatment timing
      authors: Andrew Goodman-Bacon, Journal of Econometrics 225(2)
      year: 2021
      finding: The two-way fixed effects DiD estimator with staggered timing is a weighted average of all 2x2 DiD comparisons, including ones that use already-treated units as controls; with effects that change over time those comparisons bias it.
    - id: dcdh2020
      title: Two-way fixed effects estimators with heterogeneous treatment effects
      authors: Clément de Chaisemartin, Xavier D'Haultfœuille, American Economic Review 110(9)
      year: 2020
      finding: The TWFE coefficient is a weighted sum of group-period treatment effects in which some weights can be negative.

