    figure fig aspect 3:2 members ["h13","h14","h15","rule_h","e_name","l_name","e13","e14","e15","l13","l14","l15","t_e14","t_e15","t_l15","box1","box2","c1_lab","c1","c2_lab","c2","avg","live","ctl_ok","ctl_bad","x_c2"] hidden true

    card title "Taking the estimate apart"

Where does minus one come from? With two states and three years, the regression is an average of two small difference-in-differences comparisons.
    draw h13 h14 h15 rule_h e_name l_name e13 e14 e15 l13 l14 l15

The first runs from 2013 to 2014. Early expands; Late has not yet.
    draw box1

Early gains four points and Late gains two, so the difference is two: exactly Early's true first-year effect.
    draw c1_lab c1

Late is a clean control here: in 2014 it i (@highlight l13 l14@)s still untreated, so its change is the trend alone.
    draw ctl_ok

    erase box1

The second runs from 2014 to 2015. Now Late expands, a (@highlight l15@)nd the only state left to compare it with is Ea (@highlight e14 e15@)rly, which expanded a year before.
    draw box2

Late gains four, Early gains eight, and the difference is minus four.
    draw c2_lab c2

But Early's eight is not the trend. It is two points of tr (@draw t_e14 t_e15 t_l15@)end plus six points of its own effect, still  (@highlight t_e15@)growing from two to eight.

So this comparison subtracts Early's growing effect from Late's: an already-treated state has quietly served as the control.
    draw ctl_bad x_c2

    erase box2

Here the two comparisons get equal weight, and two and minus four average to minus one, the number the regression gave.
    draw avg

And this is no quirk of two states: Andrew Goodman-Bacon showed in 2021 that any two-way fixed effects estimate with staggered timing is such a weighted average, with weights set by group sizes and by when in the panel each group switched.
    highlight c1 c2 effect underline color #e3a21a

Written in true effects instead, our beta-hat is Early's first-year effect, plus half of Late's, minus half of Early's second-year effect.
    morph avg tex "\\hat\\beta = {\\tau_{E,14}} + \\tfrac12 {\\tau_{L,15}} - \\tfrac12 {\\tau_{E,15}}"

The largest effect in the table enters with a minus sign. De Chaisemartin and D'Haultfœuille, in 2020, called these negative weights, and they come from using already-treated states as controls.
    highlight avg part "{\\tau_{E,15}}" effect underline color #e3a21a

    quiz "Suppose Early's effect had stayed at 2 in its second year, with everything else the same. What would $\\hat\\beta$ be?" intro "A new case."
        right: Two: Early's change from 2014 to 2015 is then the trend alone, so the second comparison is clean too and gives two.
        wrong: Redo the second comparison: with no growth in its effect, how much does Early gain from 2014 to 2015?
        * Minus 1, as before
        + 2, the true effect
        * 0

So the damage comes from one thing:  (@erase avg@)an effect that changes after a state has swi (@draw live ends@)tched on.
    erase x_c2 ctl_bad

So try it yourself: move Early's second-year effect, the violet eight in beta-hat's line, up and down, and watch the table and both comparisons follow.
    explore params ["b"] watch "Watch Early's second-year effect run from zero to ten, and beta-hat fall as it rises."

At two, beta-hat is two, the truth. Every point Early's effect grows takes half a point off, and at six beta-hat reaches zero.
    animate b 2 duration 2

Constant effects leave the regression honest. Effects that build, as coverage does while people enrol, turn it against itself.
    animate b 8 duration 2

## Clean controls
voice: male
level: advanced
text: {"math_hand":false}
sources:
    - id: cs2021
      title: Difference-in-differences with multiple time periods
      authors: Brantly Callaway, Pedro H. C. Sant'Anna, Journal of Econometrics 225(2)
      year: 2021
      finding: Group-time average treatment effects ATT(g,t), each estimated against never-treated or not-yet-treated units, then aggregated (e.g. by event time) with chosen weights.
      doi: 10.1016/j.jeconom.2020.12.001
    - id: sa2021
      title: Estimating dynamic treatment effects in event studies with heterogeneous treatment effects
      authors: Liyang Sun, Sarah Abraham, Journal of Econometrics 225(2)
      year: 2021
      finding: 'An interaction-weighted estimator: cohort x relative-time interactions, estimated against never-treated or last-treated units and averaged with cohort shares.'
      doi: 10.1016/j.jeconom.2020.09.006

