    figure fig aspect 3:2 members ["h13","h14","h15","rule_h","e_name","l_name","n_name","e13","e14","e15","l13","l14","l15","n13","n14","n15","att","ev_h","ev_h0","ev_h1","ev_rule","ev_r","ev_0","ev_1","ev_avg"] hidden true

    card title "Clean controls"

The repair is simple to state: never let an already-treated state play the control.
    draw h13 h14 h15 rule_h e_name l_name e13 e14 e15 l13 l14 l15

Callaway and Sant'Anna, in 2021, build the estimate from one small effect at a time: ATT of g and t, the avera (@highlight e_name l_name effect underline color #e3a21a@)ge effect for the group that expanded in year g (@highlight h13 h14 h15 effect underline color #e3a21a@), in calendar year t.
    draw att

Its missing half, the outcome without expansion, is borrowed from a state that has not been treated: one that never expands, or one that has not expanded yet.
    highlight att part "{Y_t(0)}" effect underline color #e3a21a

    quiz "In the two-state table, which effects have a clean control, a state still untreated that year?" intro "Check the table."
        right: Only Early's in 2014: by 2015 both states have expanded, so nothing untreated is left to compare with.
        wrong: In 2015 both states have expanded. Who is left untreated?
        + Only Early's in 2014
        * Early's in 2014 and Late's in 2015
        * All three

So the method estimates Early's 2014 e (@highlight e13 e14 l13 l14@)ffect against Late, which gives two, and there it stops.
    morph att tex "ATT(E,2014) = ({64} - {60}) - ({52} - {50}) = 2"

That is the first price, in its plainest form: where no clean control exists, the method reports nothing, where the regression made a number up.
    highlight e15 l15

Real expansions did have states that never expanded; Texas and Florida had still not expanded a decade later. So add on (@draw n13 n14 n15@)e, Never, gaining its two points a year.
    draw n_name

Against Never, Early gains twelve poi (@highlight e13 e15 n13 n15@)nts over two years and Never four, so Early's second-year effect is eight, the truth.
    morph att tex "ATT(E,2015) = ({72} - {60}) - ({44} - {40}) = 8"

And Late, from its last year before (@highlight l14 l15 n14 n15@) expansion, gains four against Never's two: two, the truth again.
    morph att tex "ATT(L,2015) = ({56} - {52}) - ({44} - {42}) = 2"

Then line the gr (@draw ev_h ev_h0 ev_h1 ev_rule ev_r parallel true@)oups up by years since their own expansion, not by calendar year.
    erase att

In the first year both groups show two, in the se (@draw ev_1@)cond Early shows eight, and averaging the three  (@draw ev_avg@)on purpose gives back four, not minus one.
    draw ev_0

Sun and Abraham, also in 2021, reach the same numbers from the regression side: interact each group with each event-time dummy, compare only with never-treated or last-treated states, and average with group shares.
    highlight ev_0 ev_1

The other prices are assumptions, now in plain view. Parallel trends must hold for each group against its own control, not just on average.
    highlight n13 n14 n15

No anticipation: no state may move before its start date, and expansions were announced months ahead, so insurers and hospitals had time to react.
    highlight h14 effect underline color #e3a21a

Each small comparison uses only a slice of the data, so its interval is wider than the single regression's.
    highlight ev_0 ev_1

And the choice of control is a judgment: the never-expanders, many of them in the South and a little poorer, may be the cleanest control and the least alike.
    highlight n_name effect underline color #e3a21a

## Medicaid, mortality and the older papers
voice: male
level: advanced
text: {"math_hand":false}
sources:
    - id: mjw2021
      title: 'Medicaid and mortality: new evidence from linked survey and administrative data'
      authors: Sarah Miller, Norman Johnson, Laura R. Wherry, Quarterly Journal of Economics 136(3)
      year: 2021
      finding: 'Robustness: a Goodman-Bacon decomposition puts 11% of the DD estimate on comparisons of states with different treatment times; Sun-Abraham event studies and dropping late expanders give very similar results. US citizens aged 55-64 in 2014 with low income or education (ACS linked to death records): ACA Medicaid expansions cut annual mortality by 0.132 percentage points (SE 0.050), 9.4% of the mean; event-time estimates -0.089 (year 0) to -0.208 (year 3) pp; pre-period estimates near zero. Expansions staggered 2014-2017 (21, 3, 2, 1 states).'
    - id: blw2022
      title: How much should we trust staggered difference-in-differences estimates?
      authors: Andrew C. Baker, David F. Larcker, Charles C. Y. Wang, Journal of Financial Economics 144(2)
      year: 2022
      finding: Re-analysing published staggered DiD studies in finance with robust estimators changes some of their conclusions; the authors of one re-analysed study (Fauver, Hung, Li & Taboada) reply that the re-analysis truncated their data after the last reform, and that adding three never-reform countries keeps their result.
      doi: 10.1016/j.jfineco.2022.01.004
    - id: gb2021
      title: Difference-in-differences with variation in treatment timing
      authors: Andrew Goodman-Bacon, Journal of Econometrics 225(2)
      year: 2021
      finding: The two-way fixed effects DiD estimator with staggered timing is a weighted average of all 2x2 DiD comparisons, including ones that use already-treated units as controls; with effects that change over time those comparisons bias it.
takeaways:
    - Late vs early can flip the sign
    - Build from clean comparisons
    - Check the weights, not the verdict

