    text es "11% late vs early" #3d3833 cites ["mjw2021"] x 900 y 560 font_size 40 hidden true
    text stag "21 states in 2014, 6 later" #8f887c cites ["mjw2021"] x 450 y 205 font_size 40 hidden true
    figure fig aspect 3:2 members ["yaxis","xaxis","yt0","yt1","yt2","yt3","yt4","yt5","xt0","xt2","xt4","xt6","xt8","xlab","ylab","src","zero","tline","ci0","pt0","ci1","pt1","ci2","pt2","ci3","pt3","ci4","pt4","pt5","ci6","pt6","ci7","pt7","ci8","pt8","ci9","pt9","p0","p3","dd","es","stag"] hidden true
    math mini_bad tex "\\hat\\beta = -1" size 50 x 330 y 600 hidden true
    mark mini_x around mini_bad kind strike hidden true
    math mini_good tex "ATT = 4" size 50 colors {"ATT = 4":"#8a5fa8"} x 680 y 600 hidden true
    scratch deb center cites ["blw2022"] work ["Baker, Larcker & Wang 2022:","finance results change","Fauver et al. reply:","+3 never-reform countries"] font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Medicaid, mortality and the older papers"

So are the older staggered studies wrong? One careful health study shows how to find out.
    draw yaxis xaxis xlab ylab src yt0 yt1 yt2 yt3 yt4 yt5 xt0 xt2 xt4 xt6 xt8

Miller, Johnson and Wherry linked census survey records to death records for low-income adults aged fifty-five to sixty-four, in states t (@draw tline@)hat expanded Medicaid and states that did not.
    draw zero

Before expansion, the yearly estimates sit close to zero, each well inside its interval: no sign of the groups drifting apart.
    draw ci0 pt0 ci1 pt1 ci2 pt2 ci3 pt3 ci4 pt4 pt5 parallel true

After it, annua (@draw ci6 pt6 p0@)l mortality falls, by about nine hundredths of a percentage point i (@draw ci7 pt7 ci8 pt8@)n the first year, and by about twenty-one hundredths in the fou (@draw ci9 pt9 p3 ends@)rth.

Their single difference-in-differences number is minus 0.132 points, standard error 0.050: about (@highlight dd@) nine percent of the death rate.
    draw dd

And this design has everything that went wrong in our two states: staggered timing, twenty-one states in 2014 and six later (@highlight pt6 pt7 pt8 pt9@), and an effect that grows.
    draw stag

Two things protect it. Most comparisons are against states that never expanded, and only six states came late, so already-treated states rarely serve as controls.
    highlight stag effect underline color #e3a21a

And the authors checked: a Goodman-Bacon decomposition puts only about eleven percent of their estimate on comparisons between states that expanded at different times.
    draw es

Sun and Abraham's estimator gives very similar event-time estimates, and so does dropping the la (@highlight dd@)te expanders altogether.
    highlight pt6 pt7 pt8 pt9

That is what the recent literature asks of the older papers: not that they be thrown out, but that their weights be looked at.
    highlight dd es effect underline color #e3a21a

How often the answer changes is still argued.
    fade fig to 0.2

Baker, Larcker and Wang,  (@draw deb_box deb_line_1 deb_line_2@)in 2022, re-ran published finance studies with the new estimators and found some conclusions changed.

The authors of one of them replied that the re-analysis h (@draw deb_line_3 deb_line_4@)ad cut their data short after the last reform, and that adding three countries that never reformed, clean controls, kept their result.

What settles it is checkable: the same data and the same model, run both ways, with the weight on already-treated controls shown.
    point at.ref deb_line_4 gesture underline

    erase deb

    fade fig to 1

    quiz "Every state adopts a nurse-staffing rule at some point between 2010 and 2020, and its effect on deaths builds over five years. Should you trust one two-way fixed effects number?" intro "One new case."
        right: No: with no state that never adopts, the late adopters are measured against early ones whose effects are still growing, the comparison that flipped our sign.
        wrong: With every state adopting, who is left to be the control in the last years?
        * Yes: the fixed effects absorb the timing
        * Yes, if the pre-period estimates look flat
        + No: late states are compared with early ones whose effects are still growing

So, every state gained and the regression said harm: it was not misreadin (@draw mini_bad mini_x@)g the data, it was answering a comparison nobody asked for.
    erase fig

One regression quietly averages many small comparisons, and when effects grow, the late-against-early ones can flip its sign.
    draw takeaway_1

Building from clean comparisons fixes that, at a price in data, precision and assumptions you must defend.
    draw takeaway_2 mini_good

And the older papers are not wrong by default: look at their weights, and at whether their effects grow.
    draw takeaway_3

No single regression hands us the missing outcome here: it has to be built, one clean comparison at a time.
    point at.ref mini_good gesture underline

## Where next
